# The fusions that owe no exact composed remainder. One row per (composition, composed layer) whose
# demand or nameplate sum is suspended, at the layer itself or at a node its remainder walk passes
# through; or which, at the layer itself or at a node its walk reaches, has no demand or nameplate
# figure.
#
# The restriction to two quantities is the argument
#   It is declared in the file name. `r = n - d` is a function of exactly two of the five
#   quantities composition/filed_grounds.sqlc ranges over. A suspended draw or a suspended
#   slack says nothing about the remainder and must not suspend it; a suspended demand or nameplate
#   suspends it entirely, because the correction `- e_n + e_d` then has a term nobody sized.
#
# A slice, not an aggregate
#   So this collapses `quantity`, and that is a slice, not an aggregate. The source is keyed
#   (composition, composed_layer, quantity) and this is keyed without it, which is exactly the
#   shape to watch: a σπ and a γ have the same type and make opposite claims. The claim here is the
#   σπ one and it is the right one ("either of these two is suspended") rather than "the row as a
#   whole is suspended". Reading it as the second would let a suspended slack veto a remainder that
#   is perfectly computable.
#
# A NULL `quantity` suspends every quantity
#   A NULL `quantity` is the list-level suspension. This is suspension's two levels arriving in the
#   filter itself. `eliminations/unsized` names the item and carries a quantity;
#   `eliminations/unsearched` names the list (nobody looked for double counting at all) and has no
#   per-quantity grain, so its rows come through with `quantity` NULL. An `IN` list does not match
#   NULL, so restricting to two values silently drops the suspension that is broader than either
#   of them. The arithmetic catches that where no law would, as the probe below shows.
#
# Below the layer as well as at it
#   Where a part's conversion has width, composition/remainder_frontier.sqlc walks past that part
#   into its own parts, and the corrections that node's composer applied come in through
#   composition/carried_eliminations.sqlc. If that node's own demand or nameplate sum owes nothing,
#   because nobody searched or the overlap was found and not sized, the carried correction is not a
#   number, so the remainder above it is suspended too. The walked-through nodes join the layer
#   itself as the places a suspension is looked for.
#
# And wherever a node it reaches has no figure to read
#   The pivot sums each stopping node's own remainder, and a node with no demand or nameplate figure
#   has none, so it would drop out of the sum and leave it short with nothing failing. That happens
#   where a node files one of them `derived` and its computation cannot finish, and at any stopping
#   node that files neither; composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc says which. Every such node lifts
#   the remainder above it.
#
# And at the layer itself
#   A composed layer whose own demand or nameplate has no figure has no `n - d` for its pivot to be
#   held against, so its remainder is lifted too, by the same test on the layer. One that files the
#   figure `derived` and has it computed is read at that figure (layers/demand.sqlc,
#   layers/nameplate.sqlc) and owes its remainder like any other.
#
# The filed grounds, not the whole set
#   The grounds read here are composition/filed_grounds.sqlc, which is every ground but
#   composition/underived_parts.sqlc. That one lifts a sum over a part filed `derived` whose figure
#   cannot be computed, and the walk reaches every part of a node it passes and finds no computed
#   figure there, which the arm above lifts. That arm reads composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc, so
#   the derived descent is composed here once already; reading the ground as well would add no row
#   and compose the descent a second time beneath every rule that reads a remainder.
#
# Probe
#   examples/probes runs it as `owed_remainder`. Drop the `s.quantity IS NULL` test: the complement
#   of this relation must then owe more fusions than have their demand answered (owed as a sum, or
#   filed `derived`), which is impossible, because a condition needing both quantities answered
#   cannot own more fusions than one needing demand alone.
-- composition/filed_grounds.sqlc restricted to the two quantities r is built from, at the layer or a node its walk passes;
-- composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc without a demand or nameplate figure at the layer or a node the walk reaches.
SELECT DISTINCT t.root_filing AS composition, t.root_layer AS composed_layer
FROM      (
    SELECT f.filing AS root_filing, f.layer AS root_layer, f.filing, f.layer
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/fusions.sqlc)
    ) f
    UNION ALL
    SELECT w.root_filing, w.root_layer, w.filing, w.layer
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/passed_nodes.sqlc)
    ) w
) t
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/filed_grounds.sqlc)
) s ON s.composition = t.filing AND s.composed_layer = t.layer
WHERE s.quantity IS NULL
   OR s.quantity IN ('demand', 'nameplate')
UNION
SELECT w.root_filing, w.root_layer
FROM      (
    SELECT f.filing AS root_filing, f.layer AS root_layer, f.filing, f.layer
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/fusions.sqlc)
    ) f
    UNION ALL
    SELECT r.root_filing, r.root_layer, r.filing, r.layer
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/remainder_frontier.sqlc)
    ) r
) w
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc)
) q ON q.filing = w.filing AND q.layer = w.layer
WHERE q.quantity IN ('demand', 'nameplate')
  AND q.low IS NULL
